      "text": "Since $c$ divides $\\frac{1}{5}=5^{-1}\\equiv 4\\pmod 7,$ the congruence $abc\\equiv 1\\pmod 7$ is equivalent to $\\frac{4}{c}\\cdot abc\\equiv 1\\pmod 7.$ This is just another way of writing $4a\\equiv 1\\pmod 7.$ The solution to this problem is $a\\equiv 2\\pmod 7,$ so this means $a=7+2,$ $c=7+4.$ Simplifying $6b\\equiv 3+b\\pmod 7,$ yields $5b\\equiv 3\\pmod 7$ or $2b\\equiv 3\\pmod 7.$\nThe solution to this problem is $b\\equiv 5\\pmod 7,$ so $b=7+5.$ Since $b,c,a$ are all unrelated to $7,$ these solutions are $\\boxed{13}.$ You are probably aware of a systematic method to produce every set of integers that satisfy a fixed system of congruences. $c$ obviously must be relatively prime to $5,$ and since $abc\\equiv 1\\pmod 7,$ $c$ must be a multiple of $4.$ The following lists of pairs $\\{a,b,c\\}$ with the congruence $abc\\equiv 1\\pmod 7$\n$s.csgc$ represented above tells us $c=7,$ so this solution does not satisfy the other equation. This gives $a\\equiv 2\\pmod 7$ and $b\\equiv 5\\pmod 7.$ Since $a,b,c$ are part of the set in question, $\\boxed{13}.$  We are given that $a,b,c$ are integers such that $abc\\equiv 1 \\text{ (mod } 7).$ We can then rephrase this statement as $a\\cdot b\\cdot c\\equiv 1 \\text{ (mod } 7)$. We are also given that $5c\\equiv 2 \\text{ (mod } 7).$ This implies that $c\\equiv 2\\cdot 5^{-1} \\equiv 2\\cdot 4 \\equiv 8 \\equiv 1 \\text{ (mod } 7).$ Similarly, we can solve $6b\\equiv 3+b \\text{ (mod } 7)$. This implies that $5b\\equiv 3 \\text{ (mod } 7).$ Once again, we solve for $b$ and find that $b\\equiv 5 \\text{ (mod } 7).$ Finally, since $a,b,c$ are supposed to be between $0 \\text{ and } 6,$ we combine $a\\equiv 2 \\text{ (mod } 7), b\\equiv 5 \\text{ (mod } 7),$ and $c\\equiv 1 \\text{ (mod } 7).$ Now that we know our values for $a,b,c$, we can calculate that $a+b+c\\equiv 2+5+1 \\equiv \\boxed{13}$ $\\text{ (mod } 7).$",
